│                                                                              │
│  Final Answer:                                                               │
│  - **Overall churn is 16.57%**: 841 of 5,074 customers churned.              │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Tenure is the strongest churn separator**: correlation with churn is    │
│  **-0.332**. Churned customers had **3.91** months of tenure versus          │
│  **11.4** for customers who stayed, which the tool flags as **LOWER among    │
│  churners**. The riskiest tenure bucket is **0-1 months**, with a churn      │
│  rate of **0.513**; the next buckets are far lower: **2-6 months =           │
│  0.0737**, **7-12 months = 0.0954**, **13-24 months = 0.0681**, **25+ =      │
│  0.0**.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Complaints are a major churn driver**: correlation with churn is        │
│  **0.249**. Customers who complained had a churn rate of **0.3131** versus   │
│  **0.1077** for customers with no complaints.                                │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Device registration count rises with churn**: correlation with churn    │
│  is **0.117**. Churn rates increase sharply with more registered devices:    │
│  **1 = 0.0876**, **2 = 0.0863**, **3 = 0.1452**, **4 = 0.1621**, **5 =       │
│  0.2265**, **6 = 0.3562**. The churned-vs-stayed means are not given in the  │
│  tool’s mean table, so I’m using the segment rates here.                     │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Preferred order category is highly uneven, and Mobile Phone customers   │
│  are riskiest**: churn rates by segment are **Mobile Phone = 0.2629**,       │
│  **Fashion = 0.1625**, **Laptop & Accessory = 0.105**, **Others = 0.0785**,  │
│  **Grocery = 0.0437**. Mobile Phone has the highest churn by a wide margin.  │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Payment mode separates churn strongly**: churn rates are **Cash on      │
│  Delivery = 0.2538**, **E wallet = 0.2278**, **UPI = 0.1556**, **Debit Card  │
│  = 0.1501**, **Credit Card = 0.1416**. Cash on Delivery is the riskiest      │
│  payment mode.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Satisfaction is counterintuitive: churners report higher satisfaction   │
│  scores in the overall direction summary**. Correlation with churn is        │
│  **0.101**. The churned-vs-stayed list says **SatisfactionScore: HIGHER      │
│  among churners** is not explicitly listed in the mean table, but the        │
│  segment churn rates rise from **1 = 0.1151** and **2 = 0.1263** to **5 =    │
│  0.2305**, so churn is concentrated among the highest satisfaction scores.   │
│  I’m calling this out plainly because it runs against the usual              │
│  expectation.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Cashback is lower among churners**: correlation with churn is           │
│  **-0.143**. The mean cashback amount is **161.78** for churned customers    │
│  versus **180.79** for customers who stayed, which the tool flags as         │
│  **LOWER among churners**. This aligns with a weaker-retention pattern       │
│  rather than a direct causal claim.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Single customers and City Tier 3 customers are at elevated risk**:      │
│  marital-status churn rates are **Single = 0.2672**, **Divorced = 0.1462**,  │
│  **Married = 0.113**. City-tier churn rates are **Tier 1 = 0.1397**, **Tier  │
│  2 = 0.1744**, **Tier 3 = 0.2193**. These are among the riskiest segments    │
│  in the dataset.                                                             │
