╭─────────────────────────── ✅ Agent Final Answer ────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Agent: Business Insights Analyst                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Final Answer:                                                               │
│  # Customer Churn — Business Insights                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Headline                                                                 │
│  Churn is **16.6%** overall, with **841 of 5,074 customers** leaving. The    │
│  clearest risk signal is short tenure: churn is **51.3%** in the **0–1       │
│  month** bucket, then drops to **7.4%** at **2–6 months**, **9.5%** at       │
│  **7–12 months**, **6.8%** at **13–24 months**, and **0.0%** at **25+        │
│  months**.                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Who churns                                                               │
│  Customers who churn are more likely to be early-life accounts, with         │
│  average tenure of **3.9 months** versus **11.4 months** for customers who   │
│  stayed. Churn is also higher for customers who complained: **31.3%** churn  │
│  versus **10.8%** with no complaints. Higher-risk customer segments include  │
│  **Single** customers at **26.7%**, **Divorced** at **14.6%**, and           │
│  **Married** at **11.3%**; and **City Tier 3** at **21.9%**, **Tier 2** at   │
│  **17.4%**, and **Tier 1** at **14.0%**.                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ## What drives churn                                                        │
│  Several variables are associated with churn in the same direction the EDA   │
│  found:                                                                      │
│  - **Tenure** is the strongest separator, with correlation **-0.332** and    │
│  much lower tenure among churners (**3.9 months** vs **11.4 months**).       │
│  - **Complaints** are positively associated with churn, with correlation     │
│  **0.249** and churn rates of **31.3%** vs **10.8%**.                        │
│  - **Registered devices** rise with churn, from **8.8%** at **1 device**     │
│  and **8.6%** at **2** to **35.6%** at **6 devices**.                        │
│  - **Preferred order category** is uneven: **Mobile Phone** customers churn  │
│  at **26.3%**, versus **16.3%** for **Fashion**, **10.5%** for **Laptop &    │
│  Accessory**, **7.9%** for **Others**, and **4.4%** for **Grocery**.         │
│  - **Payment mode** also separates churn: **Cash on Delivery** is highest    │
│  at **25.4%**, followed by **E wallet** at **22.8%**, **UPI** at **15.6%**,  │
│  **Debit Card** at **15.0%**, and **Credit Card** at **14.2%**.              │
│  - **Satisfaction score** is associated with higher churn, with correlation  │
│  **0.101** and churn rising to **23.1%** at score **5**.                     │
│  - **Cashback** is lower among churners: **161.78 USD** versus **180.79      │
│  USD**, with correlation **-0.143**.                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Recommended actions                                                      │
│  1. Prioritize **new customers in months 0–1** and build an early-retention  │
│  playbook, since churn is **51.3%** in that bucket.                          │
│  2. Create a fast-response workflow for **complaining customers**, because   │
│  their churn rate is **31.3%** versus **10.8%**.                             │
│  3. Target **Cash on Delivery** and **E wallet** users with retention        │
│  offers, since their churn rates are **25.4%** and **22.8%**.                │
│  4. Flag customers with **3+ registered devices**, where churn starts        │
│  rising from **14.5%** at **3 devices** to **35.6%** at **6**.               │
│  5. Review the **Mobile Phone** and **Fashion** segments first, because      │
│  their churn rates are **26.3%** and **16.3%**.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Data notes                                                               │
