╭─────────────────────────── ✅ Agent Final Answer ────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Agent: Machine Learning Engineer                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Final Answer:                                                               │
│  # Model Evaluation Report                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Summary                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  We compared three churn model variations using the same feature set and     │
│  the same fixed split. The model was selected by the highest 5-fold          │
│  cross-validated ROC-AUC on the training set, not by test performance.       │
│                                                                              │
│  By that rule, the selected model is **random_forest** with a training CV    │
│  ROC-AUC of **0.9596**. The **gradient_boosting** model was very close at    │
│  **0.9590**, and **logistic_regression** trailed at **0.8938**.              │
│                                                                              │
│  On the held-out test set, the strongest overall discrimination and          │
│  classification metrics were actually achieved by **gradient_boosting**:     │
│  - ROC-AUC: **0.9853**                                                       │
│  - PR-AUC: **0.9522**                                                        │
│  - Precision: **0.8093**                                                     │
│  - Recall: **0.9345**                                                        │
│  - F1: **0.8674**                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  However, the selected model remains **random_forest** because test results  │
│  are not allowed to influence model choice. That keeps the evaluation        │
│  honest and avoids tuning to the test set, even if another model looks       │
│  better there.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Setup                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  The dataset was split into:                                                 │
│  - Train: **4059** rows                                                      │
│  - Test: **1015** rows                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  The churn rate was very similar across splits:                              │
│  - Train churn rate: **16.6%**                                               │
│  - Test churn rate: **16.6%**                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  The feature set used **26** features. Sensitive columns **Gender** and      │
│  **MaritalStatus** were excluded from training, and fairness was checked     │
│  afterward by those groups.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Selection rule:                                                             │
│  - Pick the model with the highest **5-fold cross-validated ROC-AUC on the   │
│  training set**                                                              │
│  - Do **not** use the test set to choose the model                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Why this matters:                                                           │
│  - The test set is meant to be a final, untouched check on generalization.   │
│  - If we choose based on test results, we leak evaluation information into   │
│  model selection and make the test score overly optimistic.                  │
│  - Even if a model scores higher on test, that does not justify selecting    │
│  it after the fact.                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  One caveat in the pipeline is worth stating plainly: **Crew 1 filled        │
│  missing values with medians computed on the full dataset before the         │
│  train/test split**. That is a mild form of leakage because information      │
│  from the test distribution influences preprocessing. It is not a            │
│  catastrophic leak, but it can make results a little better than a strictly  │
│  leak-free pipeline.                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Model comparison                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Logistic regression                                                     │
│  - CV ROC-AUC: **0.8938** ± **0.0149**                                       │
│  - Test ROC-AUC: **0.8901**                                                  │
│  - Test PR-AUC: **0.6831**                                                   │
│  - Precision: **0.4778**                                                     │
│  - Recall: **0.8333**                                                        │
│  - F1: **0.6074**                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  This model is clearly the weakest of the three. It still catches many       │
│  churners, but it produces a lot of false positives relative to the other    │
│  models.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Random forest                                                           │
│  - CV ROC-AUC: **0.9596** ± **0.0070**                                       │
│  - Test ROC-AUC: **0.9802**                                                  │
│  - Test PR-AUC: **0.9259**                                                   │
│  - Precision: **0.7586**                                                     │
│  - Recall: **0.9167**                                                        │
│  - F1: **0.8302**                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  This was the selected model because it had the best cross-validated         │
│  ROC-AUC on the training set. It also generalized well on the test set.      │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Gradient boosting                                                       │
│  - CV ROC-AUC: **0.9590** ± **0.0124**                                       │
│  - Test ROC-AUC: **0.9853**                                                  │
│  - Test PR-AUC: **0.9522**                                                   │
│  - Precision: **0.8093**                                                     │
│  - Recall: **0.9345**                                                        │
│  - F1: **0.8674**                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  This model achieved the strongest test metrics across the board. But        │
│  because the test set is not used for selection, it remains a comparison     │
│  point rather than the chosen model.                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ### How to read precision and recall for a retention campaign               │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Precision** answers: of the customers we flagged for retention, how     │
│  many actually churned?                                                      │
│    - Higher precision means fewer wasted offers, coupons, or outreach        │
│  contacts.                                                                   │
│    - Lower precision means more retention spend goes to customers who would  │
│  not have churned.                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  - **Recall** answers: of the customers who actually churned, how many did   │
│  we catch?                                                                   │
│    - Higher recall means fewer missed churners.                              │
│    - Lower recall means more at-risk customers slip through without          │
│  intervention.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  For example:                                                                │
│  - **logistic_regression** has precision **0.4778** and recall **0.8333**.   │
│  That means it finds many churners, but nearly half of the flagged           │
│  customers are false positives.                                              │
│  - **random_forest** improves precision to **0.7586** while keeping recall   │
│  at **0.9167**, a much better tradeoff.                                      │
│  - **gradient_boosting** is best on both, with precision **0.8093** and      │
│  recall **0.9345**.                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  The confusion matrices tell the same story:                                 │
│  - logistic_regression: **153** false positives, **28** false negatives      │
│  - random_forest: **49** false positives, **14** false negatives             │
│  - gradient_boosting: **37** false positives, **11** false negatives         │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Selected model                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  The selected model is **random_forest**.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Why it was selected:                                                        │
│  - It had the highest 5-fold cross-validated ROC-AUC on the training set:    │
│  **0.9596**                                                                  │
│  - That is the agreed selection criterion                                    │
│  - Its CV result was also stable, with standard deviation **0.0070**, which  │
│  is reassuring                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Why not gradient_boosting, despite its stronger test metrics:               │
│  - Because the test set must not be used to pick the winner                  │
│  - The difference in test performance may be real, but it may also reflect   │
│  normal variance, the particular split, or mild leakage effects elsewhere    │
│  in the pipeline                                                             │
│  - The correct choice is still the model that won the training               │
│  cross-validation contest                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ## What drives the predictions                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  The top features by permutation importance were:                            │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Complain**: **0.0448**                                                 │
│  2. **Tenure**: **0.0261**                                                   │
│  3. **NumberOfAddress**: **0.0251**                                          │
│  4. **is_new_customer**: **0.0178**                                          │
│  5. **SatisfactionScore**: **0.0152**                                        │
│  6. **CashbackAmount**: **0.0134**                                           │
│  7. **cashback_per_order**: **0.0108**                                       │
│  8. **CityTier**: **0.0104**                                                 │
│  9. **days_since_order_per_tenure**: **0.0102**                              │
│  10. **WarehouseToHome**: **0.0095**                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Important interpretation note:                                              │
│  - Permutation importance tells us **how much a feature matters** to the     │
│  model’s predictions.                                                        │
│  - It does **not** tell us the **direction** of the effect.                  │
│  - We should not claim that a feature “increases churn” or “reduces churn”   │
│  based on importance alone.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  What this suggests at a high level:                                         │
│  - Customer complaint behavior is the strongest signal in the model.         │
│  - Tenure and new-customer status matter substantially, which is consistent  │
│  with early-life churn risk.                                                 │
│  - Order and delivery-related context, satisfaction, and cashback-related    │
│  variables also contribute meaningfully.                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Caveats                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  A few points should keep us grounded:                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **The test scores are unusually strong**                                 │
│     - ROC-AUC of **0.9802** for random forest and **0.9853** for gradient    │
│  boosting are very high.                                                     │
│     - That can happen when the signal is genuinely strong, but it can also   │
│  be inflated by dataset structure, overlap in customer behavior patterns,    │
│  or leakage.                                                                 │
│     - One known source of leakage here is the median imputation performed    │
│  on the full dataset before splitting.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  2. **The selected model is not the best on every metric**                   │
│     - Random forest won on cross-validated ROC-AUC, but gradient boosting    │
│  was better on the test set for ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, precision, recall, and F1.  │
│     - That does not change the selection rule, but it does mean the “best”   │
│  model depends on whether you mean “best by the pre-declared selection       │
│  criterion” or “best on the held-out test split.”                            │
│                                                                              │
│  3. **Importance is not causality**                                          │
│     - A high permutation importance score does not mean a feature causes     │
│  churn.                                                                      │
│     - It only means the model relies on that feature when making             │
│  predictions.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  4. **Fairness metrics show different behaviors by group**                   │
│     - For **Gender**:                                                        │
│       - Female: customers **404**, actual churn rate **18.6%**, flagged      │
│  rate **22.8%**, recall **85.3%**                                            │
│       - Male: customers **611**, actual churn rate **15.2%**, flagged rate   │
│  **18.2%**, recall **96.8%**                                                 │
│     - For **MaritalStatus**:                                                 │
│       - Divorced: customers **157**, actual churn rate **17.2%**, flagged    │
│  rate **20.4%**, recall **96.3%**                                            │
│       - Married: customers **540**, actual churn rate **11.5%**, flagged     │
│  rate **16.9%**, recall **95.2%**                                            │
│       - Single: customers **318**, actual churn rate **24.8%**, flagged      │
│  rate **25.2%**, recall **87.3%**                                            │
│                                                                              │
│     These numbers do not prove bias by themselves, but they do show that     │
│  model behavior differs across groups and should be monitored before         │
│  deployment.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Overall, the project produced a strong churn classifier, and the selected   │
│  **random_forest** model is defensible under the stated selection rule. At   │
│  the same time, the unusually high test results and the preprocessing        │
│  leakage mean we should treat the metrics as encouraging, not as proof that  │
│  the model will perform identically in production.                           │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

